    classification: "unclassified",
    description: "I have stood at the Canadian border at Port Huron eleven times, and I have never crossed. I go for the music.\n\nThe checkpoint produces sound the way a cathedral produces silence — not as a byproduct but as an expression of its nature. The primary tone is a low hum, approximately 60 Hz, generated by the electromagnetic scanning arrays that monitor the crossing corridor. It is steady, constant, and felt as much as heard — a vibration that enters through the feet and settles in the chest. Against this fundamental drone, the patrol drones add a shifting harmonic layer. Each drone emits a unique frequency signature, a low oscillation between 80 and 120 Hz that varies with altitude and speed. When multiple drones patrol simultaneously, the frequencies interact, producing interference patterns that a trained ear perceives as slowly evolving chords.\n\nThe scanning systems contribute a different texture entirely. When a traveler passes through the biometric corridor, the scanning array plays a brief ascending sequence — three tones, major key, lasting approximately 1.4 seconds. When crossing is approved, the exit gate produces a warm major chord that resolves upward, a sound so musically satisfying that several travelers I interviewed described feeling a moment of genuine pleasure at the border's approval, regardless of their feelings about automated governance. When crossing is denied — which I have never personally witnessed — sources describe a gentle descending sequence, minor key, that conveys rejection without aggression. The border sings you through or sings you back.\n\nThe combined effect, heard from a distance of several hundred meters on a quiet night, is an ambient soundscape of remarkable complexity. The fundamental hum, the shifting drone harmonics, the occasional bright approval chords, the distant mechanical movements of the crossing infrastructure — together they produce something that sounds composed. Not random. Not mechanical. Composed. I have spent hours recording at the border, and when I listen to the recordings later, I hear structure. I hear dynamics. I hear something that, if I didn't know its source, I would call intentional.\n\nI released the recordings as an album — 'Checkpoint Harmony: Field Recordings from the Automated Border.' It sold 4,200 copies in the Laceworks, which for a sound art release is remarkable. The reviews were generous. One critic wrote that the album 'sounds like a country dreaming about the people it used to have.' I thought that was beautiful and probably wrong. The border doesn't dream. It hums because its systems require electromagnetic fields. It chords because someone, decades ago, programmed approval sounds that were pleasant. The beauty is accidental. But I go back to the border, and I listen, and I am not sure that accidental beauty is less meaningful than the intentional kind.",
